  "problem": "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nI. $A∧B$\n\nII. $A∧(B∨¬A)$\n\nIII. $¬(¬B∨¬A)$",
  "worked_solution": "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A∧(B∨¬A)\\,$ | $\\,¬(¬B∨¬A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nWe see that the statements $A∧B,\\,$ $A∧(B∨¬A)$ and $¬(¬B∨¬A)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
